Ladybird keeps a track buffer’s exact frame ranges and, alongside them,
the coalesced ranges it reports as buffered, updating both as frames
arrive and removing an evicted group’s ranges in one pass. The other
engines also keep their coalesced view current, rather than rebuilding
it from every frame’s range on each query. Chromium’s SourceBufferStream
merges a buffer into a SourceBufferRange that it falls within the fudge
room of (SourceBufferRange::CanAppendBuffersToEnd(), GetFudgeRoom()),
and evicts a whole group at a time with DeleteGOPFromFront(). Gecko’s
TrackBuffersManager keeps its exact mBufferedRanges beside the separate
mSanitizedBufferedRanges, which bridges gaps up to the longest frame
duration, and RemoveFrames() subtracts the removed intervals together.
And WebKit’s TrackBuffer::addSample() adds coverage with the option
EliminateSmallGaps, while TrackBuffer::removeSamples() collects all the
erased ranges and then subtracts them together.
